#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199091} :activity/id #uuid "8bc28183-1c22-5cc0-8df5-bd16b54f088d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048592 :topic/id #uuid "ac50c7f3-cf1f-565d-96af-32bd8ce3b7b9" :topic/math-academy-id 4254} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199541 :tutorial/content "Recall that a *predicate* of one variable is a sentence that contains a variable defined on some universal set and has the property that when we substitute a concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a mathematical statement that is either true or false.\n\nThe **truth set** $T$ of a predicate $P(x)$ is the set of elements in its universal set $U$ such that substituting these elements into the predicate gives a true statement:\n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{ x \\in U \\: | \\: P(x) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's find the truth set of the following predicate:\n\n$\\qquad$ $D(x):$ $x$ is divisible by $2,$\n\nwhere the universal set is $U = \\big\\{ 1,2, \\ldots, 6 \\big\\}.$\n\nTo find the truth set $T,$ we need to find $x \\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ $x$ is divisible by $2.$\n\nLet's check all the elements in our universal set $U$ to determine which of them are elements of the truth set $T{:}$\n\n- $x=1\\notin T$ since $1$ is *not* divisible by $2$\n\n- $x=2\\in T$ since $2$ is divisible by $2$\n\n- $x=3\\notin T$ since $3$ is *not* divisible by $2$\n\n- $x=4\\in T$ since $4$ is divisible by $2$\n\n- $x=5\\notin T$ since $5$ is *not* divisible by $2$\n\n- $x=6\\in T$ since $6$ is divisible by $2$\n\nTherefore, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{ 2,4,6 \\big\\}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8422ca20-ab2c-5e41-a789-3c21465dfc51" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8139 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "dbb18ea5-8c55-58c8-81e5-4c923006baa2" :step/math-academy-id 153521 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199092}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199092 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198279 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198096 :question/id #uuid "4b253527-f5ca-5927-97f0-d91491d598b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12721"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c23caadd-1738-549f-aade-2c983dc250dc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050073}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575593}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Truth Set of a Predicate"} :step/id #uuid "3cbfca6e-271d-50e8-8a2d-2854b810036b" :step/math-academy-id 153522 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199093}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199093 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199540 :tutorial/content "A predicate of the variables $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ is a sentence that contains these variables with the property that when we substitute concrete values for all the variables, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false. \n\nWe can denote such a predicate using the following notation:\n\n$$\nP(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)\n$$\n\nIf $U_1$ is the universal set for $x_1,U_2$ is the universal set for $x_2,\\ldots,$ etc, then the universal set $U$ for our predicate is given by the Cartesian product\n\n$$\nU = U_1 \\times U_2 \\times \\cdots \\times U_n.\n$$\n\nFor example, the sentence\n\n$\\qquad$ $P(x,y): x+y \\lt 2,$ $\\quad$ $x,y \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\nis a predicate of two variables, $x$ and $y.$ In this case, the universal set is\n\n$$\nU = \\mathbb{R} \\times \\mathbb{R} = \\mathbb{R}^2.\n$$\n\nThe **truth set** $T$ of a predicate $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is the set of $n$-tuples $(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n) \\in U$ such that substituting these tuples into the predicate gives a true statement:\n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{ (x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\in U \\: | \\: P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor example, to determine the truth set of the predicate $P(x,y)$ defined above, we need to find $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb R^2$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ $x+y \\lt 2$\n\nTo find the truth set, we first find an explicit condition on $y$ as a function of $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & <2 \\\\ y & <2-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the truth set contains all tuples $(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2$ that satisfy this inequality. We can write the truth set $T$ of our predicate as follows:\n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\:|\\: y < 2-x\\big\\}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $T$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^2,$ we can represent it as a region in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/3e/3ebd1253b4980e2be41247128a426412adafe34a64fdc38d2d7141cb20d62354.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "5cec17b5-8507-56d1-8683-8c2a82af211d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8138 :tutorial/title "Predicates With More Than One Variable"} :step/id #uuid "aef558ff-96f1-5037-bf03-b3799da39d24" :step/math-academy-id 153525 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199094}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199094 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198280 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198097 :question/id #uuid "14dc17f1-056e-5e82-81e6-c3964b7df2d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-12722"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa2f2ccb-45b0-5d6a-aa94-ac3eb95c745b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575590}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Predicates of More Than One Variable"} :step/id #uuid "fc98c6d5-6d1d-5d6a-9c44-fa2bf85d1072" :step/math-academy-id 153526 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199095}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199095 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198281 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198098 :question/id #uuid "3d212cb2-394b-5f9d-8e7d-8406ec7ca580" :question/math-academy-id "e-12723"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f9703bd-92ae-51f7-902b-251a7947ba6a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051063}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575605}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Truth Set of a Multivariable Predicate"} :step/id #uuid "99a08b9b-5aec-5987-b707-883afcf5ea45" :step/math-academy-id 153529}] :activity/title "Truth Sets of Predicates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198513}]}
